def compress(text):
    letters = [ch for ch in text.casefold() if ch.isalpha()]
    parts = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(letters):
        j = i
        while j < len(letters) and letters[j] == letters[i]:
            j += 1
        run = j - i
        parts.append(letters[i] if run == 1 else f'{letters[i]}{run}')
        i = j
    return ''.join(parts)

Normalisation and encoding are separate steps, so each is easy to reason about. The inner loop only moves `j` forward and `i` jumps to `j`, so every character is visited once: O(n) time and O(n) space for the parts. Building a list and joining once keeps the output construction linear instead of relying on `+=`. The empty case needs no special branch: the while loop never runs and `''.join([])` is `''`. `itertools.groupby(letters)` is an equivalent one-liner once you know it.

